# {{ project_name }}

A ROS 2 {{ ros_distro }} development environment in a VS Code dev container
{%- if ardupilot %}, with an ArduPilot simulation (Mission Planner, SITL and MAVROS){% endif %}.

## Get started

```bash
./scripts/setup_dev_env.sh
sudo reboot
code .
```

When VS Code finishes loading, click **Reopen in Container** in the bottom right corner.

Put your ROS packages under `src/`. When the container is created, the workspace is built automatically if it has
packages and hasn't been built yet. apt's package lists are fetched too, so `sudo apt install` works without an update
first and package names tab-complete.

`~/.bashrc` in the container sources ROS{% if uv_venv %}, the uv venv{% endif %} and the workspace overlay
(`install/setup.bash`). VS Code takes its environment from it when it connects, so extensions see the same
environment as the terminal. After building new packages, run **Developer: Reload Window** so extensions pick them up.

## Commands

The recipes are in `justfile` and `just/`; run `just` to list them.

| Command          | Description                                                     |
| ---------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `just deps`      | Install the dependencies of the packages in `src/` with rosdep |
| `just build`     | Build the workspace with colcon                                 |
| `just test`      | Run the workspace tests                                         |
| `just clean`     | Remove `build/`, `install/` and `log/`                          |
| `just lint`      | Run all linters on all files                                    |
{%- if ardupilot %}
| `just sim-start` | Start the simulation and follow its logs in a tmux session      |
| `just sim-kill`  | Stop the simulation                                             |
{%- endif %}

## Linting

Linters run through [pre-commit](https://pre-commit.com), which pins each tool's version in
`.pre-commit-config.yaml`. The dev container installs the git hook, so they run on every commit made inside it.
Commits made outside the container need `pre-commit` installed on the host.

- Python: ruff (lint and format), mypy
- Shell: shellcheck, shfmt
- Dockerfiles: hadolint
- YAML: yamllint; GitHub workflows: actionlint; TOML: tombi; justfiles: `just --fmt`
- General: codespell, whitespace, file endings, JSON, XML and merge-conflict checks

Tool settings are in `ruff.toml`, `mypy.ini`, `.yamllint.yaml`, `.hadolint.yaml`, `.codespellrc` and `.editorconfig`.
Packages that declare ament linter tests (`ament_lint_auto`) still run them as part of `just test`.
{%- if uv_venv %}

## Python dependencies

Python packages that aren't available through rosdep go in `pyproject.toml`, and `uv add <package>` adds one. uv
installs them into `.venv`, which is created with `--system-site-packages` so the ROS Python packages stay
importable. Terminals activate it automatically, and both `just build` and `colcon` in a terminal run colcon from it,
so the nodes' entry points use the venv's interpreter.

- A package in `pyproject.toml` that's also installed through apt (`numpy`, for example) takes precedence over the apt
  version in the venv. ROS packages that your nodes import were built against the apt version, and can break.
- rosdep keys that resolve to pip packages are installed with the system pip, not into the venv. Add those with
  `uv add` instead.
{%- endif %}
{%- if claude_code %}

## Claude Code

Claude Code is installed in the container. Its authentication and settings are kept in the `claude-config` docker
volume, so they survive container rebuilds. Your host `~/.claude` isn't shared with the container.
{%- endif %}
{%- if ardupilot %}

## Simulation

`just sim-start` starts the simulation containers from inside the dev container and opens a tmux window with each
one's logs; `just sim-kill` stops them. ArduPilot SITL (Copter {{ ardupilot_version }}) simulates the vehicle with its
built-in physics, MAVROS connects to it, and forwards MAVLink to Mission Planner.
{%- if ardupilot_gazebo %}

`just sim-gazebo-start` starts the same containers with SITL flying in Gazebo through the
[ardupilot_gazebo](https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot_gazebo) plugin (the `iris_runway.sdf` world), by layering
`docker/compose/simulation/docker-compose.gazebo.yml` on `docker-compose.yml`. The `gazebo` and `sitl` containers share
a network, so the plugin and SITL talk over 127.0.0.1.
{%- endif %}
{%- if gazebo_vm_rendering %}

Gazebo renders with Ogre 1 through the host's `/dev/dri`, for VMs such as VMware Fusion or Parallels on Apple Silicon
where Ogre 2 doesn't work.
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}
